PRSL-AI ECOSYSTEM
CANONICAL PERMISSIONS MODEL

Organization: Robert Software Labs
Status: Canonical
Version: 1.0
Classification: Core Architecture
Document Type: Architectural Permission & Access Control Standard

==================================================
1. PURPOSE
==================================================

This document defines the canonical Permissions Model of the
PRSL-AI Ecosystem.

It establishes:

- How PRSL-AI decides what may be accessed.
- Who grants authorization.
- How permissions are represented.
- How permissions are scoped.
- How permissions are revoked.
- How temporary permissions work.
- How different access levels are represented.
- How Identity, Authorization, Privacy, Security and Governance
  interact.
- How permission decisions remain auditable.

==================================================
2. CORE PRINCIPLE
==================================================

Identity answers:

"WHO IS THE ACTOR?"

Authentication answers:

"CAN THE ACTOR PROVE THAT IDENTITY?"

Authorization answers:

"IS THIS ACTOR ALLOWED TO PERFORM THIS OPERATION?"

Permission answers:

"WHAT EXACTLY MAY THIS ACTOR ACCESS OR DO?"

Privacy answers:

"UNDER WHAT CONDITIONS MAY THE DATA BE PROCESSED?"

Governance answers:

"WHICH SYSTEM RULES APPLY?"

Therefore:

IDENTITY
≠
AUTHENTICATION
≠
AUTHORIZATION
≠
PERMISSION
≠
PRIVACY
≠
GOVERNANCE

==================================================
3. PERMISSION DEFINITION
==================================================

A Permission is an explicit authorization to perform a defined
operation over a defined resource under defined conditions.

Canonical structure:

SUBJECT
+
ACTION
+
RESOURCE
+
SCOPE
+
CONDITIONS
=
PERMISSION

Example:

USER_001
+
READ
+
MEMORY
+
PERSONAL_MEMORY
+
AUTHENTICATED_SESSION
=
AUTHORIZED

==================================================
4. WHO GRANTS PERMISSION?
==================================================

The ultimate authority for user-controlled permissions is the
user.

However, permission decisions may also be constrained by:

- System Security.
- Governance.
- Privacy Policy.
- Device Security.
- Organizational Policy.
- Integration Policy.
- Capability Policy.

Therefore:

USER GRANTS AUTHORITY

but:

SYSTEM POLICY DEFINES THE BOUNDARIES

A user cannot grant a permission that the architecture
fundamentally prohibits.

==================================================
5. PERMISSION DECISION MODEL
==================================================

A permission decision should evaluate:

IDENTITY
↓
AUTHENTICATION
↓
RESOURCE
↓
ACTION
↓
SCOPE
↓
USER AUTHORIZATION
↓
PRIVACY POLICY
↓
SECURITY POLICY
↓
GOVERNANCE
↓
DEVICE / SESSION STATE
↓
FINAL DECISION

Result:

ALLOW
DENY
REQUIRE_CONFIRMATION
REQUIRE_REAUTHENTICATION
REQUIRE_ELEVATION
UNKNOWN

==================================================
6. DEFAULT DENY
==================================================

PRSL-AI follows:

DEFAULT DENY

unless access has been explicitly authorized or is explicitly
defined as safe public/system functionality.

The absence of a permission must never be interpreted as:

ALLOW.

==================================================
7. LEAST PRIVILEGE
==================================================

Permissions must provide the minimum access required for an
operation.

Example:

An AI component needs to read one document.

It should receive:

READ
+
DOCUMENT_123

rather than:

READ
+
ENTIRE_FILE_SYSTEM

Canonical principle:

ACCESS ONLY WHAT IS REQUIRED.

==================================================
8. PERMISSION GRANULARITY
==================================================

Permissions may be scoped at multiple levels:

1. Ecosystem
2. Account
3. Device
4. Application
5. Capability
6. Resource
7. Resource type
8. Action
9. Session
10. Task
11. Time
12. Data classification

Example:

READ
→ Photos
→ Selected Album
→ Current Session

This is more restrictive than:

READ
→ All Photos
→ Permanent Access

==================================================
9. ACCESS LEVELS
==================================================

PRSL-AI may represent access using canonical levels.

LEVEL 0
NO ACCESS

LEVEL 1
DISCOVERY

LEVEL 2
METADATA ACCESS

LEVEL 3
READ ACCESS

LEVEL 4
WRITE ACCESS

LEVEL 5
EXECUTION ACCESS

LEVEL 6
ADMINISTRATIVE / CONTROL ACCESS

The exact levels may be capability-specific.

Higher access must not automatically imply every lower-level
permission unless explicitly defined by the capability contract.

==================================================
10. NO ACCESS
==================================================

LEVEL 0:

NO_ACCESS

The actor cannot:

- Read.
- Modify.
- Execute.
- Enumerate protected information.

The resource may be completely invisible.

==================================================
11. DISCOVERY ACCESS
==================================================

LEVEL 1:

DISCOVER

The actor may determine that a capability or resource exists,
without receiving protected content.

Example:

"Is there a printer available?"

The system may respond:

Printer available.

Without exposing:

Printer configuration.

==================================================
12. METADATA ACCESS
==================================================

LEVEL 2:

READ_METADATA

Example:

File:

photo.jpg

Metadata:

- Filename.
- Size.
- Creation date.

But not necessarily:

Image contents.

==================================================
13. READ ACCESS
==================================================

LEVEL 3:

READ

The actor may access resource content.

Example:

READ_DOCUMENT

This does not automatically imply:

WRITE
DELETE
SHARE
EXECUTE

==================================================
14. WRITE ACCESS
==================================================

LEVEL 4:

WRITE

The actor may modify the resource.

Write access does not automatically imply:

DELETE

or:

ADMINISTER

unless explicitly defined.

==================================================
15. EXECUTION ACCESS
==================================================

LEVEL 5:

EXECUTE

The actor may trigger an operation.

Examples:

- Send message.
- Start automation.
- Run integration action.
- Execute system operation.

Execution may require additional verification.

==================================================
16. ADMINISTRATIVE ACCESS
==================================================

LEVEL 6:

ADMINISTER

Allows control over protected configuration or authorization
relationships.

Examples:

- Grant permission.
- Revoke permission.
- Register integration.
- Change security configuration.

Administrative access is highly restricted.

==================================================
17. RESOURCE OWNERSHIP
==================================================

Ownership and permission are separate concepts.

Example:

USER_001 owns:

MEMORY_123

A system component may have:

READ

without becoming:

OWNER

Ownership remains with the appropriate domain or user.

==================================================
18. USER PERMISSIONS
==================================================

A user may authorize PRSL-AI to access specific resources.

Examples:

ALLOW CAMERA

ALLOW MICROPHONE

ALLOW LOCATION

ALLOW CONTACTS

ALLOW FILES

ALLOW MEMORY

ALLOW EXTERNAL_PROCESSING

These permissions must be independently representable.

==================================================
19. DEVICE PERMISSIONS
==================================================

A device may have its own permission boundary.

Example:

USER_001:

CAN_USE_CAMERA

Device:

PHONE_A

Camera:

AVAILABLE

But:

TABLET_B

Camera:

DENIED

The user identity is the same.

Device permission differs.

==================================================
20. APPLICATION / CAPABILITY PERMISSIONS
==================================================

Permissions may be granted to a specific capability rather than
to the entire ecosystem.

Example:

Vision Capability:

READ_SELECTED_IMAGE

rather than:

Vision Capability:

READ_ALL_FILES

This reduces unnecessary authority.

==================================================
21. AI AGENT PERMISSIONS
==================================================

AI agents must not inherit unlimited user authority.

Example:

Calendar Agent

may have:

READ_CALENDAR

WRITE_CALENDAR

But not:

READ_PHOTOS

unless explicitly required and authorized.

Canonical principle:

AI INTELLIGENCE DOES NOT EQUAL UNLIMITED AUTHORITY.

==================================================
22. PERMISSION DELEGATION
==================================================

A permission may be delegated.

Example:

User
↓
@iriel
↓
Calendar Agent

The agent receives:

READ_CALENDAR

The agent does not become the owner of the calendar.

Delegated authority must retain:

- Original authority.
- Scope.
- Expiration.
- Purpose.
- Revocation path.

==================================================
23. SCOPED PERMISSIONS
==================================================

Permissions should be scoped whenever practical.

Examples:

READ:

Calendar

Scope:

TODAY

or:

READ:

Files

Scope:

/Documents/Project-A

or:

WRITE:

Messages

Scope:

Conversation_123

==================================================
24. TEMPORARY PERMISSIONS
==================================================

Permissions may be temporary.

Example:

ALLOW_LOCATION

Duration:

30 minutes

After expiration:

DENIED

Temporary permission must automatically expire.

==================================================
25. ONE-TIME PERMISSIONS
==================================================

A permission may be valid for a single operation.

Example:

ALLOW_CAMERA

Scope:

ONE_IMAGE

After capture:

REVOKED

This is particularly valuable for sensitive data.

==================================================
26. SESSION-BOUND PERMISSIONS
==================================================

A permission may exist only during a session.

Example:

ALLOW_FILE_ACCESS

Scope:

CURRENT_SESSION

Session ends:

Permission expires.

==================================================
27. TASK-BOUND PERMISSIONS
==================================================

A permission may exist only while completing a task.

Example:

Task:

"Organize my downloaded files."

Permission:

READ_DOWNLOADS
WRITE_DOWNLOADS

After:

TASK_COMPLETED

The temporary authorization may expire.

==================================================
28. PURPOSE-BOUND PERMISSIONS
==================================================

Permissions may specify their intended purpose.

Example:

READ_CAMERA_IMAGE

Purpose:

"Analyze image requested by user."

The same permission should not automatically authorize:

"Upload image to external service."

==================================================
29. CONTEXTUAL PERMISSIONS
==================================================

Permission decisions may depend on Context.

Example:

Location permission:

ALLOW

while:

USER_IS_NAVIGATING

but:

DENY

for unrelated background processing.

Contextual permissions must remain predictable and transparent.

==================================================
30. SENSITIVE DATA PERMISSIONS
==================================================

Sensitive data requires stronger controls.

Examples:

- Health-related information.
- Financial information.
- Authentication information.
- Private communications.
- Highly personal images.
- Biometric-related data.

Access may require:

- Explicit consent.
- Reauthentication.
- User confirmation.
- Local processing.
- Additional governance checks.

==================================================
31. EXTERNAL PROCESSING PERMISSION
==================================================

External AI processing is a distinct permission boundary.

Example:

USER:

Allow PRSL-AI to analyze image.

This does NOT automatically mean:

Allow PRSL-AI to send image to external provider.

These are separate permissions.

Canonical distinction:

LOCAL_PROCESSING

versus:

EXTERNAL_PROCESSING

==================================================
32. CLOUD PERMISSION
==================================================

Cloud access should be represented separately where applicable.

Example:

ALLOW_CLOUD_STORAGE

does not automatically mean:

ALLOW_EXTERNAL_AI_PROCESSING

or:

ALLOW_THIRD_PARTY_SHARING

Each data movement must have its own policy.

==================================================
33. PERMISSION AND PRIVACY
==================================================

Permission alone is insufficient.

Example:

User grants:

ALLOW_EXTERNAL_AI

But the data is classified:

HIGHLY_SENSITIVE

Privacy policy may still require:

DENY

or:

REQUIRE_CONFIRMATION

Therefore:

PERMISSION
+
PRIVACY
+
SECURITY
+
GOVERNANCE

determine the final outcome.

==================================================
34. PERMISSION AND SECURITY
==================================================

Security can override a permission when required.

Example:

User authorized:

WRITE_SYSTEM_CONFIGURATION

Security detects:

Compromised Session

Result:

DENY

The permission remains conceptually valid, but execution is
blocked because the current security state is unsafe.

==================================================
35. PERMISSION AND GOVERNANCE
==================================================

Governance can impose system-level restrictions.

Example:

User:

ALLOW_EXTERNAL_PROCESSING

Governance:

External processing prohibited for this data classification.

Result:

DENY

The user permission does not override a higher-priority policy.

==================================================
36. PERMISSION PRIORITY
==================================================

Canonical decision hierarchy:

SECURITY
↓
GOVERNANCE
↓
PRIVACY
↓
USER AUTHORIZATION
↓
CAPABILITY POLICY
↓
DEVICE / SESSION CONDITIONS

The exact implementation may use a policy engine, but a lower
priority permission must not override a mandatory higher-level
restriction.

==================================================
37. PERMISSION STATE
==================================================

A permission may have states:

REQUESTED
PENDING
GRANTED
DENIED
EXPIRED
REVOKED
SUSPENDED
CONSTRAINED

These states must be distinguishable.

==================================================
38. GRANTED
==================================================

GRANTED means:

The permission currently satisfies all required authorization
conditions.

It does not necessarily mean that every future operation will
be allowed.

Context, security, privacy and governance may change.

==================================================
39. DENIED
==================================================

DENIED means:

The requested authorization is not currently available.

Possible reasons:

- User denied.
- Policy denied.
- Security denied.
- Privacy denied.
- Capability unavailable.
- Device restriction.

The reason should be represented internally for diagnostics.

==================================================
40. EXPIRED
==================================================

EXPIRED means:

The permission was valid but its defined lifetime ended.

Example:

30-minute location permission.

After 30 minutes:

EXPIRED.

==================================================
41. REVOKED
==================================================

REVOKED means:

Previously granted authority has been intentionally withdrawn.

Revocation must take effect according to the security guarantees
of the system.

==================================================
42. SUSPENDED
==================================================

SUSPENDED means:

The permission remains conceptually configured but is temporarily
inactive.

Example:

Device security state compromised.

Permission:

SUSPENDED

After recovery:

May return to GRANTED.

==================================================
43. CONSTRAINED
==================================================

CONSTRAINED means:

Access is allowed but under reduced scope.

Example:

READ_PHOTOS

becomes:

READ_SELECTED_PHOTO

==================================================
44. PERMISSION REVOCATION
==================================================

Permissions must be revocable.

Canonical flow:

GRANTED
↓
REVOCATION REQUEST
↓
AUTHORIZATION UPDATE
↓
ACTIVE ACCESS TERMINATED
↓
REVOKED
↓
AUDIT EVENT

==================================================
45. IMMEDIATE REVOCATION
==================================================

Sensitive permissions should support immediate revocation.

Example:

User:

"Stop letting iRiel access my location."

Result:

LOCATION_PERMISSION
→ REVOKED

New requests:

DENIED

Existing authorization tokens must be invalidated according to
the system's security guarantees.

==================================================
46. DEVICE REVOCATION
==================================================

If a device is revoked:

All device-bound permissions should be re-evaluated.

Example:

DEVICE_REVOKED

Results:

Camera permission:
REVOKED ON DEVICE

Memory access:
REVOKED ON DEVICE

Cloud session:
REVOKED

User Identity:

REMAINS ACTIVE

==================================================
47. SESSION REVOCATION
==================================================

When a session is revoked:

Session-bound permissions become invalid.

Example:

SESSION_REVOKED

→ temporary permissions expire immediately.

Long-lived identity permissions may remain unaffected.

==================================================
48. DELEGATED PERMISSION REVOCATION
==================================================

If the user revokes a permission delegated to an AI agent:

User
↓
@iriel
↓
Agent

Permission:

READ_CONTACTS

Revoked.

Agent must no longer be able to use that authority.

==================================================
49. PERMISSION TOKENS
==================================================

Implementations may represent permissions through signed or
cryptographically protected tokens.

A token should contain only required information.

Possible fields:

PERMISSION_ID
SUBJECT_ID
RESOURCE
ACTION
SCOPE
ISSUED_AT
EXPIRES_AT
POLICY_VERSION
AUTHORITY
STATUS

Tokens must not become an alternative source of truth for
identity.

==================================================
50. PERMISSION ID
==================================================

Every permission assignment should have a unique identifier.

Example:

perm_01XYZ

This allows:

- Revocation.
- Auditing.
- Versioning.
- Tracing.
- Delegation tracking.

==================================================
51. PERMISSION VERSIONING
==================================================

Permissions may change over time.

Example:

Permission v1:

READ_ALL_PHOTOS

Permission v2:

READ_SELECTED_PHOTOS

The system must be able to determine which authorization was
active at a particular time.

==================================================
52. PERMISSION AUDIT
==================================================

Important permission events should be auditable.

Examples:

PERMISSION_REQUESTED
PERMISSION_GRANTED
PERMISSION_DENIED
PERMISSION_REVOKED
PERMISSION_EXPIRED
PERMISSION_SUSPENDED
PERMISSION_SCOPE_CHANGED

Audit records should include:

- Permission ID.
- Subject.
- Resource.
- Action.
- Decision.
- Authority.
- Timestamp.
- Reason / policy reference where appropriate.

==================================================
53. PERMISSION EVENT MODEL
==================================================

The Permission Model integrates directly with the Event Model.

Example:

PERMISSION_GRANTED
↓
Event Router
↓
Relevant subsystem

Later:

PERMISSION_REVOKED
↓
Event Router
↓
Sessions / Agents / Capabilities
↓
Access terminated

Consumers must process revocation events idempotently.

==================================================
54. PERMISSION CACHE
==================================================

Permissions may be cached for performance.

However:

A cache must not become more authoritative than the permission
source of truth.

Sensitive revocations must propagate within defined security
guarantees.

==================================================
55. OFFLINE PERMISSIONS
==================================================

PRSL-AI may operate offline.

Therefore, some permissions may be evaluated locally.

The system must define:

- Locally valid permissions.
- Expiration.
- Revocation guarantees.
- Required online verification.
- Security fallback.

Offline operation must never silently expand authority.

==================================================
56. PERMISSION FAILURE
==================================================

If permission status cannot be reliably determined for a
sensitive operation:

DEFAULT:

DENY

or:

REQUIRE_REAUTHENTICATION

or:

REQUIRE_CONFIRMATION

depending on the operation.

UNKNOWN must not silently become ALLOW.

==================================================
57. PERMISSION AND VERIFICATION
==================================================

Permission answers:

"May this operation occur?"

Verification answers:

"Did the operation actually occur as expected?"

Example:

Permission:

ALLOW_SEND_MESSAGE

Execution:

ATTEMPTED

Verification:

FAILED

The permission remains distinct from the execution result.

==================================================
58. PERMISSION AND ROUTER
==================================================

The Intelligence Router must consider permissions before
selecting an execution path.

Example:

User asks:

"Analyze this private image."

Router sees:

Local Vision:

AUTHORIZED

External Vision:

NOT AUTHORIZED

Result:

Select Local Vision.

The Router must not route to an unauthorized provider merely
because it has greater capability.

==================================================
59. PERMISSION AND @IRIEL
==================================================

@iriel does not possess unlimited authority.

@iriel operates using:

- User permissions.
- Capability permissions.
- Context.
- Privacy rules.
- Security policies.
- Governance.

Therefore:

@IRIEL INTELLIGENCE
≠
UNLIMITED USER AUTHORITY

==================================================
60. PERMISSION AND MEMORY
==================================================

Memory access must be permission-aware.

Example:

Core AI may have:

READ_RELEVANT_MEMORY

This does not automatically mean:

READ_ALL_MEMORY.

Sensitive Memory may require additional authorization.

==================================================
61. PERMISSION AND KNOWLEDGE
==================================================

Knowledge may have different access levels.

Example:

Public Knowledge:

READ

Private Knowledge:

READ with user authorization

Restricted Knowledge:

READ only for specific capability

Knowledge permissions must preserve source restrictions.

==================================================
62. PERMISSION AND INTEGRATIONS
==================================================

Integrations require explicit permission boundaries.

Example:

Calendar Integration:

READ_CALENDAR

WRITE_CALENDAR

Email Integration:

READ_EMAIL

SEND_EMAIL

The presence of an integration does not automatically authorize
every operation.

==================================================
63. EXAMPLE — CAMERA
==================================================

User asks:

"Tire uma foto."

Flow:

Identity:
Authenticated

Device:
Camera available

Permission:
CAMERA = GRANTED

Privacy:
No additional restriction

Governance:
Allowed

Result:

ALLOW

==================================================
64. EXAMPLE — PRIVATE IMAGE
==================================================

User asks:

"Analise esta imagem privada."

Permission:

READ_SELECTED_IMAGE = GRANTED

Local Vision:

AUTHORIZED

External Vision:

DENIED

Result:

Local processing.

No external upload.

==================================================
65. EXAMPLE — MESSAGE
==================================================

User:

"Envie uma mensagem para João."

Permission:

SEND_MESSAGES = GRANTED

But:

Verification requirement:

YES

Flow:

Authorize
↓
Execute
↓
Verify
↓
Report result

Permission does not imply successful delivery.

==================================================
66. EXAMPLE — CONTACTS
==================================================

User:

"Quem é João?"

Capability needs:

READ_CONTACTS

Permission:

DENIED

Result:

PRSL-AI cannot access Contacts.

It may ask the user for permission.

==================================================
67. EXAMPLE — TEMPORARY ACCESS
==================================================

User:

"Use minha localização para encontrar restaurantes próximos."

Permission:

LOCATION

Scope:

CURRENT_REQUEST

Result:

Temporary permission.

After request:

EXPIRED

Future unrelated location access:

DENIED

==================================================
68. EXAMPLE — REVOKED ACCESS
==================================================

User previously allowed:

READ_PHOTOS

Later:

"Não quero mais que o iRiel veja minhas fotos."

Result:

PERMISSION_REVOKED

Existing access:

TERMINATED according to security guarantees.

Future requests:

DENIED

==================================================
69. EXAMPLE — COMPROMISED DEVICE
==================================================

Device security state:

COMPROMISED

Permission:

WRITE_SYSTEM_CONFIGURATION = GRANTED

Security:

BLOCK

Result:

PERMISSION EFFECTIVELY SUSPENDED

The user's configured authorization remains recorded but cannot
be exercised while the security condition is unsafe.

==================================================
70. EXAMPLE — AI AGENT
==================================================

User asks:

"Organize meus arquivos."

File Agent receives:

READ:
Downloads

WRITE:
Downloads

It does NOT receive:

READ:
Photos

READ:
Messages

READ:
Health Data

The Agent receives only the authority required for the task.

==================================================
71. PERMISSION CONSENT
==================================================

Consent must be meaningful.

The system should communicate:

- What is being accessed.
- Why it is needed.
- For how long.
- Which scope applies.
- Whether data leaves the device.
- Whether an external provider is involved.

The user should not need to understand the internal architecture
to make an informed decision.

==================================================
72. PERMISSION TRANSPARENCY
==================================================

The system should make active permissions inspectable.

Example:

iRiel Permissions

Camera:
Allowed for current task

Location:
Not allowed

Memory:
Relevant memories only

External AI:
Not allowed for sensitive data

This gives the user operational visibility.

==================================================
73. PERMISSION MANAGEMENT
==================================================

Users should be able to:

- View permissions.
- Grant permissions.
- Deny permissions.
- Revoke permissions.
- Change scope.
- Change duration.
- Review delegated permissions.
- Review external integrations.

==================================================
74. PERMISSION OWNERSHIP
==================================================

The permission itself is a system authorization object.

The underlying resource may belong to:

- User.
- Device.
- Integration.
- System.
- Organization.

Permission grants access.

It does not transfer ownership.

==================================================
75. PERMISSION CONSISTENCY
==================================================

Permission state must have an authoritative source.

Caches, tokens and local projections are derived.

When permissions change:

AUTHORITATIVE STATE
↓
EVENT
↓
AUTHORIZED CONSUMERS
↓
LOCAL PROJECTIONS

==================================================
76. PERMISSION INVARIANTS
==================================================

The following are canonical:

1. Default is DENY unless explicitly allowed.

2. Identity does not imply permission.

3. Authentication does not imply authorization.

4. Authorization does not override Privacy.

5. User permission does not override mandatory Security or
   Governance constraints.

6. Permissions must be scoped.

7. Permissions must be revocable.

8. Temporary permissions must expire.

9. One-time permissions must not become persistent.

10. AI agents must receive least privilege.

11. External processing requires an explicit policy boundary.

12. Device permissions are independent from User Identity.

13. Permission state must be auditable.

14. Revocation must propagate according to defined guarantees.

15. UNKNOWN must never silently become ALLOW.

16. Permission tokens are not the canonical identity.

17. Permission does not imply successful execution.

18. Permission does not imply ownership.

19. Sensitive operations may require reauthentication or
    confirmation.

20. Every permission must have a defined authority and scope.

